WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:04.434
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy Award-winning comedian,

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:06.300
who is in his record-breaking
20th season on

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.601
"Saturday Night Live."

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:09.968
The show returns live on
NBC and Peacock this Saturday

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:13.067
with host Aubrey Plaza
and musical guest Sam Smith.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:14.200
Welcome back to the show

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.400
the one, the only
Kenan Thompson, everybody.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.467
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:37.434 --> 00:00:39.000
Hello, my friend.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.033
-Wassup?!
-So, 20 seasons, Kenan.

00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:45.834
-Is that not crazy?
-It's crazy.

00:00:45.834 --> 00:00:46.901
-It's crazy.

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:48.467
I don't know
what to do with that.

00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:49.934
-Yeah.

00:00:49.934 --> 00:00:53.467
You're coming up on
as many years in this building

00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:54.934
as out of this building.

00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:59.601
-Yeah. It's -- Like, at this
point, the show is 48 years old.

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:02.267
-Yeah.
-I've been there for 20.

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:03.400
You know what I mean?
-Yeah, exactly.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.601
-It is insane to me,
because "SNL" was --

00:01:06.601 --> 00:01:08.300
you know, it was everything
growing up, and then --

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.200
-At the 50th anniversary
of "SNL,"

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.934
they'll do a montage
of through the years.

00:01:11.934 --> 00:01:13.367
It could just be you.

00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:15.100
-It's going to be a lot of me.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.567
That's wild to me, so, yeah.

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:18.400
-Now, it's a trip, 'cause you
were how old when you started?

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.634
24 years old?
-25, yeah.

00:01:19.634 --> 00:01:21.067
-25 years old.

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:24.601
And, at the same time, you came
in as a very young person,

00:01:24.601 --> 00:01:26.701
but you were also this
accomplished sketch actor.

00:01:26.701 --> 00:01:29.968
So I think --
-Yes, I was. Yes, I was.

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:31.067
-You know what I mean? Like...

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:33.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:33.634 --> 00:01:36.367
-And why I say that is,
it's a full 20.

00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:38.501
Like a lot -- I mean, I feel
like the first three years

00:01:38.501 --> 00:01:40.300
I was there,
if you squeezed it all together,

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.934
would be like
two months of sketches,

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:44.467
whereas you were
so useful immediately

00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:47.267
and so good at it immediately,
you were a big, important

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:48.267
cog in the machine
from the jump.

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.434
-Thank you, man.

00:01:49.434 --> 00:01:50.968
I mean, it's a lot of work,
but, you know,

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:52.801
that's what we came there to do?

00:01:52.801 --> 00:01:55.100
Question mark.
-Yeah.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.934
Now, I'm very happy to say
that I am, of course,

00:01:57.934 --> 00:02:02.200
not the only one who agrees
about your incredible talent.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.033
And you just got this
over in Hollywood,

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:06.801
the Hollywood Walk of Fame.
-Oh, look at that.

00:02:06.801 --> 00:02:08.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:08.734 --> 00:02:10.300
-There you go.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.534
Now, how was the ceremony?

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:16.400
I know our friends
Leslie Jones, J.B. Smoove

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.467
were out there for you.

00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:19.400
-Yes, and my good buddy
Josh Server.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.033
They all spoke from the heart,

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:25.200
and it was a beautiful, warm,
I think, August day.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.601
-Oh, that's -- Yeah.
-In Los -- Yeah.

00:02:26.601 --> 00:02:28.734
Yeah, 'cause that is --
You are -- You know, for people

00:02:28.734 --> 00:02:31.033
that haven't been there,
it's also still a full street.

00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:32.501
-Yes, it is a full
Hollywood Boulevard.

00:02:32.501 --> 00:02:34.267
-So a hot August day in
Los Angeles.

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:36.501
-Yeah, with a whole lot of,
"Hey, Kenan!"

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:39.133
from across the street.

00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:42.300
And, you know, it was
a very touching day, you know?

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.634
-They say that you're
the easiest to recognize

00:02:44.634 --> 00:02:46.634
when you're kneeling next to
a star with your name on it.

00:02:46.634 --> 00:02:48.267
-1,000%, yeah.

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:51.000
-Did you check to see what
the adjacent stars were?

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.300
That's the part that is
always interesting to me.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.400
-Crazy enough, I saw an "L-O,"
'cause the carpet

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.334
was covering one,
and then I saw, like, an "L-S."

00:03:00.334 --> 00:03:01.467
So I couldn't see the full name.

00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:03.367
I was like,
"Is that Lorne Michaels?"

00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:05.167
And I'm right next to
Lorne Michaels.

00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:08.601
-Really? You're on the
Lorne Michaels block.

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:13.234
-Like, literally,
like, touching stars.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:14.467
It's...

00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:16.200
-And you know he made sure
he got the best part

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.300
of Hollywood Boulevard.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.534
-1,000%.

00:03:18.534 --> 00:03:21.334
Yeah, no, we are actually
further down the street

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:24.100
than actual concrete
handprints.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.834
And it was funny --
when I told Leslie that,

00:03:25.834 --> 00:03:28.968
she's like, "Why are you all
the way down here?"

00:03:28.968 --> 00:03:32.300
-Leslie is like, "I'm going to
give my speech down there

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.434
if you want to come down,

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:35.000
and then we'll walk back
to your star."

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.067
Hey, another cool thing
happened this year

00:03:38.067 --> 00:03:39.133
when Keke Palmer
was on the show...

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:40.334
-Whew!

00:03:40.334 --> 00:03:43.734
-..and you guys did
a "Kenan &amp; Kel" sketch,

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:44.934
and Kel showed up.

00:03:44.934 --> 00:03:47.067
-Yes.
-And this just --

00:03:47.067 --> 00:03:49.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Amazing.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.501
Look, it looks like --
Does that not look like '96?

00:03:53.501 --> 00:03:54.534
-Yeah.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:57.033
Everyone is aging very well.

00:03:57.033 --> 00:03:59.567
-Yeah.
That black is not cracking.

00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:01.033
-No.

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:05.868
If you showed a Nickelodeon show
with white people from 1996,

00:04:05.868 --> 00:04:09.968
it would be -- the audience
would have screamed.

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:11.067
-You said it.

00:04:11.067 --> 00:04:12.901
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:14.300
-But this was --
Was this Keke's idea?

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:17.701
-That was a very special,
special week, because --

00:04:17.701 --> 00:04:19.133
Yes, it was Keke's idea.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:20.934
She told me about it on Monday
and she was like,

00:04:20.934 --> 00:04:22.801
"I got an idea for the show" --
"Kenan and Kelly."

00:04:22.801 --> 00:04:26.234
And I was like, "Say no more.
Like, I get it 1,000%."

00:04:26.234 --> 00:04:29.267
And I was trying to figure out
how to make, you know,

00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:31.167
Nickelodeon appealing for
the "SNL" audience.

00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:32.501
You know what I mean?

00:04:32.501 --> 00:04:34.868
Because it's like child
kind of network or whatever,

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:36.901
and this is adult humor.

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:38.534
And this was
the first time I thought

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:40.367
we would be able to
figure that out.

00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:42.701
So we did,
and then, like, you know,

00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:44.801
her tag line,
you know, was just, like,

00:04:44.801 --> 00:04:47.834
off from Kel's tag line,
"Oh, here it goes."

00:04:47.834 --> 00:04:49.834
So, then we thought like,
what's the most

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:51.467
nonsensical kind of thing
she could say

00:04:51.467 --> 00:04:52.901
that is still kind of real?

00:04:52.901 --> 00:04:56.300
And we came up with
"Uh-oh, here comes the bus."

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.300
And by the time
she did it the third time,

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.334
it was just, like,
oh, priceless.

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:02.434
-Everybody here
is really cast --

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:03.968
I do also want to
point out, Mikey Day --

00:05:03.968 --> 00:05:06.067
perfect in that world.

00:05:06.067 --> 00:05:08.000
Like, in another world,

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.634
Mikey Day is definitely on
a Nickelodeon show.

00:05:10.634 --> 00:05:11.834
-1,000%.

00:05:11.834 --> 00:05:15.801
Or headed towards one now.

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:18.234
But, yeah, Mikey playing
Dan Fishman was great.

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:20.267
And then Kel came in
and just destroyed it.

00:05:20.267 --> 00:05:21.801
You know what I mean?
It was very special.

00:05:21.801 --> 00:05:24.701
-It's a really cool thing,
which I recognize

00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:26.033
we're talking about this --

00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:29.033
usually, the people on the show,
when the host comes in,

00:05:29.033 --> 00:05:30.834
they're like, "Oh, I have
this idea from this thing

00:05:30.834 --> 00:05:32.067
I saw you in years ago."

00:05:32.067 --> 00:05:35.067
This is the first time
I've heard about a host

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:37.133
coming in and saying,
"I want to do something"...

00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:38.367
-From your past. Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah, from your past.

00:05:38.367 --> 00:05:39.434
-Right. Yeah.

00:05:39.434 --> 00:05:41.000
-But I think it speaks --
Again, it speaks

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.334
to the remarkable sense
not just that you've

00:05:42.334 --> 00:05:43.467
been on "SNL" for 20 years,

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:45.300
but you had this
real career beforehand.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.367
-Yeah, man, it's been a crazy
kind of whirlwind experience.

00:05:48.367 --> 00:05:50.801
I don't really know, like, how
to put it in words, necessarily,

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:53.133
but I am very grateful,
because it was emotional

00:05:53.133 --> 00:05:54.267
even for, like, the crew.

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:55.400
You know what I mean?

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:56.934
Like, "This brings me back
to my childhood."

00:05:56.934 --> 00:05:58.467
Like, everybody was saying that.

00:05:58.467 --> 00:06:02.167
And walking onto that set
in a place

00:06:02.167 --> 00:06:03.667
that, you know,
I've been knowing for 20 years

00:06:03.667 --> 00:06:04.901
is something else.

00:06:04.901 --> 00:06:06.033
You know what I mean?

00:06:06.033 --> 00:06:07.434
It was such
a blast from the past.

00:06:07.434 --> 00:06:09.801
It was amazing.
So I'm beyond blessed.

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:11.467
-You've also been there so long,
one of the things --

00:06:11.467 --> 00:06:12.634
You know, I went through it,
as well.

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:13.634
The longer you are there,
the more you have to say

00:06:13.634 --> 00:06:14.834
goodbye to people.

00:06:14.834 --> 00:06:18.133
You said goodbye to the great
Cecily Strong.

00:06:18.133 --> 00:06:19.434
Left the show
the Christmas show.

00:06:19.434 --> 00:06:20.667
And there you are with
Austin Butler.

00:06:20.667 --> 00:06:22.934
-One time for Cecily.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:22.934 --> 00:06:26.434
-It must be emotional
when people leave.

00:06:26.434 --> 00:06:28.667
I remember my last show,
you couldn't stop crying.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:30.467
Do you remember that?
You were just sobbing.

00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:33.467
-Yeah. I mean,
everybody was just, like --

00:06:33.467 --> 00:06:35.100
couldn't move when you left.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.067
It was unbelievable. I remember.

00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:40.234
I'm laughing about it now only
because it's been so long.

00:06:40.234 --> 00:06:42.200
-Hey, I got a lot more to
ask you.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.367
Will you stick around?
-Absolutely.

00:06:43.367 --> 00:06:45.767
-We'll be right back
with more from the great

00:06:45.767 --> 00:06:47.467
Kenan Thompson, everybody.

